  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 344 AM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES-East Nighttime Microphysics RGB imagery shows \ndeep clouds associated with a disturbance remaining mostly over \nWisconsin, though the MNM METAR does show BKN028 at 0645Z. \nElsewhere, fair-weather clouds or clear conditions are observed. \nKMQT radar shows the UP clear of precipitation as of 07Z but some \nscattered weak reflectivities have been observed just over the state \nline from Gogebic County as well as widespread weak reflectivities a \ncounty or two from Menominee County and moving east-northeast. \nRecent CAMs have been a bit more aggressive in bringing very light \nshowers across much of the central UP today, but with the best \nforcing for showers well to the south, will keep PoPs and QPF low \nfor this forecast package. A slight upwards trend in the temperature \nforecast for this morning has resulted in this forecast trimming \nback the frost potential to just some patches of the eastern third \nof the UP, so no frost advisory will be issued.\n\nThe 500mb pattern consists of 595 dam ridging over northeast TX \nbounded by deep troughs centered over Idaho, Florida, and Hudson \nBay. A ridge-riding shortwave over the WI/IL state line is causing \nthe aforementioned disturbed weather to the south. While models have \nlong-since trended precipitation with that disturbance primarily \nsouth of the UP, the Hudson trough will pass a weak cold front \nacross the region for Sunday. A surge of cooler air aloft will cause \nmarginally-supportive thermodynamic profiles for some lake-enhanced \nor lake-effect rainfall, with CAMs showing the orographic forcing of \nthe western UP being enough to force weak precipitation. Thus, some \nlight PoPs and a couple hundredths of an inch of QPF have been added \nfor the north/western UP for Sunday. This will also come with some \ngustier NE winds, with gusts of 20+ MPH forecast especially along \nthe Lake Superior shores. This will also drive waves of up to 6 ft \nwithin 5 miles of the Lake Superior shoreline, which will \ndrastically increase the risk of hazardous rip currents and \nchallenging swimming conditions for those trying to squeeze in a \nlate-September Sunday beach day at Lake Superior beaches.\n\nIn the wake of the weekend disturbances, the western CONUS trough \nwill shift over the Dakotas by Sunday, weakening slightly. At the \nsame time, ridging to the north of the trough amplifies, causing \nsplit flow. This results in dominant surface high pressure \nestablishing over northern Ontario and Quebec through much of next \nweek. The NBM reintroduces PoPs to the forecast late next Saturday, \nbut that might be down to ensemble chaos more than an actual precip \nsignal. Upstream ridging aloft results in cooler continental flow \naloft and being on the south/southwest periphery of a surface high \nresults in cool easterly to northeasterly flow at the surface off of \nthe Canadian Shield, which will lead to cool conditions at the \nbeginning of the week with a gradual warming trend as the week goes \non. For instance, the NBM even calls for up to 50% chances of sub-\nfreezing temperatures for Tuesday morning, though the LREF chances \nare significantly lower. By the end of the week, NBM highs are \nwidespread 70+. The CPC 6-10 and 8-14 day outlooks both show 50+% \nchances for the UP to be in above average temperatures for the end \nof September and a slight favoring of below normal precipitation (30-\n40% chance).",
